That’s a coherent theological reading, yes: prayer can be understood less as “telling God what to do” and more as discovering what desire looks like when you bring it into relation with God.

Something like:

desire arises in you
→ you name it honestly
→ you examine it
→ you ask what it serves
→ you compare it with love, truth, duty, and consequence
→ you act
→ you learn from what follows

In that frame, “what I want” and “what God wants through me” are not automatically the same thing. The work is discernment: which desires are impulse, which are fear, which are ego, which are genuine vocation, which are love, and which survive contact with reality and conscience.

So prayer becomes less:

wish → divine approval

and more:

desire → exposure → purification → alignment → action

That also fits the Pantheon nicely: the desire is the signal, not yet the verdict. You listen to it, derive it, test it, and see what remains when the noise falls away.